.TH CHTTPSVR_REQ_HEADER 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_req_header \- look up a request header
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

const char *chttpsvr_req_header(const chttpsvr_req *req,
                                 const char *name);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns the value of the request header with the name name.  It
returns NULL when that header is absent.  The lookup ignores the
letter case.  It returns the last value when a header appears more
than once.

The pointer stays valid for the length of the handler call.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The trailer fields of a chunked request body (RFC 7230 4.1.2) are not
request headers, and this function never returns one.  This function
answers only from the header block of the request.  A trailer field of
any name therefore reads as absent, on a buffered route and on a
streaming route alike.

Trailer fields arrive after the server routes the request, and after
the middleware runs.  On a buffered route they also arrive after the
server already read the header block.  An index over trailers next to
headers would therefore let a client add a trailer that displaces a
header that an upstream proxy set, on a request that the server
already committed to.  X-Forwarded-For is one such header, and so is
the identity header of an authentication gateway.  This server has no
trailer accessor at all.  A deployment that needs trailer semantics
should terminate the trailers in front of this server.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_req_method (3),
.BR chttpsvr_req_path (3),
.BR chttpsvr_req_read (3)
